---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/drip-vs-brevo';
const title = 'Drip vs Brevo for Shopify SMS Marketing (2026)';
const desc = 'Compare Drip and Brevo for Shopify drops, SMS eligibility, event identity, permission, purchase exits and campaign costs.';
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "When does Drip fit?",
    "answer": "Drip can fit an ecommerce team already operating email journeys that wants eligible SMS inside those workflows. Confirm SMS approval, US scope, paid-plan and Support All People requirements, and email-based identity before committing."
  },
  {
    "question": "When does Brevo fit?",
    "answer": "Brevo can fit a team coordinating email and contact operations with eligible SMS. Confirm recipient countries, paid-account eligibility, credits and sender approval; verify the purchased Shopify PushOwl integration separately from a standalone Brevo account."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does syncing a phone number authorize a campaign?",
    "answer": "No. Map Shopify customer identity and retain permission evidence for each channel and purpose. Email permission, transactional contact details and promotional SMS permission are separate."
  },
  {
    "question": "Will a purchase stop queued messages?",
    "answer": "Verify the actual event and workflow configuration. Test order arrival during a wait, duplicate events, delayed delivery and replay before relying on purchase exits across tools."
  },
  {
    "question": "Do all Brevo channels and CRM workflows come with every plan?",
    "answer": "No. Check the current marketing plan, automation limits, Sales add-ons and channel eligibility for the exact actions required. A CRM contact or deal is not proof of SMS permission or Shopify inventory."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should costs and outcomes be compared?",
    "answer": "Use comparable eligible-customer groups and a holdout. Calculate net sales after discounts and returns, then subtract goods, fulfillment, platform, messaging and support costs once. Choose a test window from purchase and return cycles, not a fixed pilot duration."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-14">
    <nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a> / Drip vs Brevo</nav>
    <header class="max-w-4xl">
      <p class="badge">Campaign marketing comparison</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Drip vs Brevo for Shopify SMS marketing</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 text-xl">Choose around the journeys your team can operate, the countries you serve and the permissions you can prove. Neither tool is a universal winner.</p>
    </header>
    <TldrBox title="Conditional campaign fits" picks={[
      { label: 'Drip', tool: 'Drip', reason: 'Ecommerce email journeys with SMS where approved and enabled' },
      { label: 'Brevo', tool: 'Brevo', reason: 'Email and contact operations with eligible channels and verified Shopify integration scope' },
    ]} />
    <article class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Channel scope before workflow design</h2>
      <p>Drip supports SMS within its ecommerce marketing mix; describing it only as an email layer misses that option. Its <a class="link" href="https://www.drip.com/sms">SMS access page</a> describes staged access for existing US customers on paid plans with Support All People enabled. Confirm account approval and recipient coverage before scheduling a drop. The <a class="link" href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/48924286539661-Quick-Glance-SMS-with-Drip">SMS quick guide</a> explains setup; verify how phone records attach to email-based profiles rather than assuming a phone-only import can enter every journey.</p>
      <p>Brevo offers email, SMS, WhatsApp and customer relationship tools, but availability is not a promise that every channel or workflow is included in a base account. Check <a class="link" href="https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/208589409-About-Brevo-s-pricing-plans">plan and add-on scope</a>, paid-account eligibility for SMS, credits, sender registration and <a class="link" href="https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/208717449-Supported-countries-and-pricing-for-SMS-messages">recipient-country restrictions</a>. Marketing automation and Sales CRM actions have their own entitlements. Do not treat WhatsApp approval, transactional sending or a deal pipeline as promotional SMS access.</p>
      <p>For Shopify, inspect the current <a class="link" href="https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/360014912879-Brevo-PushOwl-app-for-Shopify-Connect-your-Shopify-store-with-Brevo-PushOwl">Brevo PushOwl connection</a> and its purchased scope. Record which customer fields and events actually reach the account used for sending. A standalone Brevo feature list does not establish what the installed app syncs.</p>
      <h2>Identity, permission and purchase exits</h2>
      <p>Map Shopify customer ID, normalized phone, email, order ID and event time to the receiving profile. Test changed emails, shared numbers, guest checkout and profile merges. Keep the store authoritative for orders, subscription state and stock; keep an explicit owner for channel permission and suppression. Email consent does not grant SMS consent, and an order phone number is not a marketing opt-in. Retain source, timestamp, disclosure and purpose with revocations.</p>
      <p>Before a release, test a purchase arriving while a message waits, an opt-out after audience selection, an out-of-order event and a duplicate or replayed order. Recheck permission, stock, frequency and purchase status at dispatch. Use event IDs to prevent duplicate entry and reconcile Shopify orders against processed events and sends. Quarantine unmatched identities and stale inventory rather than silently retrying them into a promotional flow. Assign one operator to investigate failures and one sender for each message job.</p>
      <p>Apply destination-specific sending rules and recipient-local quiet hours with unknown-timezone handling. Test opt-out and help handling for each sender type. Propagate suppression according to its channel and purpose; document when a broader request suppresses all marketing without conflating that with necessary transactional messages. Review <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/tcpa">sending-rule guidance</a> for the markets involved.</p>
      <h2>Proposed merchant workflow tests</h2>
      <p>The following are illustrative planning scenarios retained from the original page, not customer case studies or executed results. Set audience thresholds, frequency limits and observation windows from actual stock, permission and buying cycles.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Arena Energy:</strong> pair an athlete flavor launch with a gym-bag interest segment. Keep the shaker offer in email and reserve eligible SMS for release timing; verify both messages agree on availability.</li>
        <li><strong>Bloom &amp; Wick:</strong> plan holiday teasers and shipping deadlines separately for gift buyers and self-purchasers. Test bundle SMS eligibility versus scent education email and stop deadline messages when fulfillment cannot meet the promise.</li>
        <li><strong>Peak Trail Gear:</strong> isolate clearance offers to bargain-sensitive RFM segments. Compare against a holdout and check whether full-price loyalists need an incentive at all.</li>
        <li><strong>Sauce Lab:</strong> trigger batch scarcity only from fresh bottle inventory. Test a restrained launch and follow-up policy, including cancellation when stock is exhausted.</li>
        <li><strong>Ember Ceramics:</strong> preserve pottery waitlist priority through a small kiln release. Pair a maker-story email with an eligible access text and verify queue order survives retries.</li>
        <li><strong>Ridge Run Coffee:</strong> exclude active subscribers from non-subscriber flash offers. Test a subscription change between audience selection and dispatch.</li>
        <li><strong>Cipher Audio:</strong> coordinate a teaser email and launch text to eligible engaged browsers. Compare full-price access with an offer using contribution margin rather than claiming a historical lift.</li>
        <li><strong>Patchwork Denim:</strong> match one-of-one listing drops to style preferences. Test sold-item removal and prevent duplicate claims before comparing tailored and broad audiences.</li>
        <li><strong>Loom &amp; Frame:</strong> disclose print edition size and target repeat collectors at a verified sell-through milestone. Reconcile inventory before sending scarcity language.</li>
        <li><strong>Driftwood Surf Co:</strong> coordinate swell-forecast content with seasonal boardshort drops. Compare permissioned restock waitlists against a broader eligible audience without assuming either performs better.</li>
        <li><strong>Noir Collective:</strong> coordinate a recurring streetwear release with earlier VIP access. Test lower broadcast frequency and measure total margin and opt-outs, not only revenue per send.</li>
        <li><strong>SoleDrop ATX:</strong> preserve sneaker waitlist priority by size. Release general-list email only when stock supports it; verify that early access cannot oversell a size run.</li>
        <li><strong>Festival Threads Co:</strong> align city-specific tour merchandise with venue dates and recipient timezones. Test city exclusions and a store-defined contact cap across overlapping dates.</li>
        <li><strong>Vinyl Vault Records:</strong> disclose pressing quantity and give eligible waitlisted repeat collectors first access. Define value thresholds from store data and test access without automatically adding discounts.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Economics and message ownership</h2>
      <p>Compare quotes for the same peak-month audience, recipient countries and encoded message lengths. Include plan fees, SMS segments and carrier charges, sender setup, automation or CRM add-ons, integration work and support. Avoid assuming unlimited contacts means unlimited workflow capacity or included SMS. Read <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">pricing economics</a> alongside current vendor quotes.</p>
      <p>For a proposed holdout, compare contribution per eligible customer over equivalent purchase and return windows. Start with net sales after discounts and returns; subtract goods, fulfillment, platform, messaging and support costs once. Incremental contribution is the treated-group contribution per eligible customer minus the comparable holdout value. Keep holdout exclusions synchronized and distinguish attributed sales from incremental lift.</p>
      <p>Choose Drip if its approved channels and store-connected journeys meet the tested release needs. Choose Brevo if its purchased Shopify integration, channel coverage and contact or CRM operations meet them. Either may own lifecycle email; adding Sequenzy requires a distinct job and shared suppression, not an automatic extra layer. Consider a separate SMS sender only for a verified gap. Document capture ownership, inventory authority, purchase exits, retries, reconciliation and rollback before changing senders.</p>
    </article>
    <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Drip vs Brevo FAQ" />
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
